am Protocol), we must look at their fundamental design philosophies: **TCP is designed for reliability**, while **UDP is designed for speed and efficiency**.\n\nHere is a detailed comparison based on your specific criteria.\n\n### 1. Guarantees\n\n| Feature | TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) | UDP (User Datagram Protocol) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Reliability** | **Guaranteed.** TCP ensures that data is delivered exactly as sent. It uses acknowledgments (ACKs) and retransmissions. | **No guarantee.** UDP sends packets without checking if they arrived. It is \"fire and forget.\" |\n| **Ordering** | **Guaranteed.** Packets are delivered in the exact order they were sent. | **No guarantee.** Packets may arrive out of order or not at all. |\n| **Connection** | **Connection-oriented.** A handshake (SYN/SYN-ACK/ACK) is required before data transfer begins. | **Connectionless.** No handshake is required; data is sent immediately. |\n| **Flow Control** | **Yes.** TCP manages congestion and ensures the receiver isn't overwhelmed.", "client": {"ttft_s": 0.6842304000019794, "decode_s": 4.903818399994634, "total_s": 5.588394099999277, "n_gen": 256, "n_chunks": 256, "tg": 52.00029430133037, "pp": 43.84488032088784}}
